{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"EXT_ID:14617643 AND SRC:MED","internalQuery":"EXT_ID:14617643 AND SRC:MED","resultType":"CORE","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":25,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"14617643","source":"MED","pmid":"14617643","pmcid":"PMC262710","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC262710"]},"doi":"10.1128/jb.185.23.6790-6800.2003","title":"New bacterial pathway for 4- and 5-chlorosalicylate degradation via 4-chlorocatechol and maleylacetate in Pseudomonas sp. strain MT1.","authorString":"Nikodem P, Hecht V, Schlömann M, Pieper DH.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Nikodem P","lastName":"Nikodem","firstName":"Patricia","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Environmental Microbiology, GBF-German Research Center for Biotechnology, D-38124 Braunschweig, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Hecht V","lastName":"Hecht","firstName":"Volker","initials":"V"},{"fullName":"Schlömann M","lastName":"Schlömann","firstName":"Michael","initials":"M"},{"fullName":"Pieper DH","lastName":"Pieper","firstName":"Dietmar H","initials":"DH"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data","related_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"23","volume":"185","journalIssueId":1173835,"dateOfPublication":"2003 Dec","monthOfPublication":12,"yearOfPublication":2003,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Bacteriol","title":"Journal of bacteriology","essn":"1098-5530","issn":"0021-9193","isoabbreviation":"J Bacteriol","nlmid":"2985120R"},"printPublicationDate":"2003-12-01"},"pubYear":"2003","pageInfo":"6790-6800","abstractText":"Pseudomonas sp. strain MT1 is capable of degrading 4- and 5-chlorosalicylates via 4-chlorocatechol, 3-chloromuconate, and maleylacetate by a novel pathway. 3-Chloromuconate is transformed by muconate cycloisomerase of MT1 into protoanemonin, a dominant reaction product, as previously shown for other muconate cycloisomerases. However, kinetic data indicate that the muconate cycloisomerase of MT1 is specialized for 3-chloromuconate conversion and is not able to form cis-dienelactone. Protoanemonin is obviously a dead-end product of the pathway. A trans-dienelactone hydrolase (trans-DLH) was induced during growth on chlorosalicylates. Even though the purified enzyme did not act on either 3-chloromuconate or protoanemonin, the presence of muconate cylcoisomerase and trans-DLH together resulted in considerably lower protoanemonin concentrations but larger amounts of maleylacetate formed from 3-chloromuconate than the presence of muconate cycloisomerase alone resulted in. As trans-DLH also acts on 4-fluoromuconolactone, forming maleylacetate, we suggest that this enzyme acts on 4-chloromuconolactone as an intermediate in the muconate cycloisomerase-catalyzed transformation of 3-chloromuconate, thus preventing protoanemonin formation and favoring maleylacetate formation. The maleylacetate formed in this way is reduced by maleylacetate reductase. Chlorosalicylate degradation in MT1 thus occurs by a new pathway consisting of a patchwork of reactions catalyzed by enzymes from the 3-oxoadipate pathway (catechol 1,2-dioxygenase, muconate cycloisomerase) and the chlorocatechol pathway (maleylacetate reductase) and a trans-DLH.","affiliation":"Department of Environmental Microbiology, GBF-German Research Center for Biotechnology, D-38124 Braunschweig, Germany.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Comparative Study","Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Pseudomonas","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Maleates","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"